	doi = {10.1093/jmt/25.3.145},
	abstract = {Investigated the effects of 2 components of group singing (music and activity) on trust and cooperation, using 96 adult Israeli males (aged 22–41 yrs). Relationships between (a) music and trust and (b) activity and cooperation were predicted. Group singing was expected to yield the highest trust and cooperation scores. Ss participated in a single session of one of the following activities: group singing (music/activity), listening to music (music/no activity), poetry reading (no music/activity), and film viewing (no music/no activity). Ss also played a prisoner's dilemma game and completed a trust differential questionnaire. Results confirm the predictions for the effects of music on trust and of activity on cooperation. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)},
