	title = {Enhancement of {Disaster} {Preparedness}: {Approaches} of {Place} {Attachment} and {Behavior} to “{Build} {Back} {Better}” {Mosque} as {Tsunami} {Evacuation} {Building} in {Banda} {Aceh} {City}, {Indonesia}},
	shorttitle = {Enhancement of {Disaster} {Preparedness}},
	url = {https://papers.ssrn.com/abstract=4432114},
	doi = {10.2139/ssrn.4432114},
	abstract = {The Sendai Framework for Disaster Risk Reduction in 2015-2030 emphasized the importance of enhancing preparedness and "Build Back Better" through risk reduction during the recovery, rehabilitation, and reconstruction phases. Aceh Province, Indonesia, was one of the worst areas hit by a devastating earthquake and tsunami. This led to a simulation analysis by Mc. Caughey in 2017, with 50\% of the respondents in Banda Aceh City indicating their preference for mosque as tsunami evacuation site in the future. This study aims to identify the feasibility of using mosque as a tsunami evacuation building. Data on community attachment was collected through participatory observation and in-depth interviews, guided by the Theory of Behavior and the Concept of Place Attachment. The results showed that Baitul Musyahadah Mosque was the preferred choice that suits the requirements of a tsunami evacuation building. People expressed preferences for mosque based on factors such as building form (38\%), horizontal accessibility (22\%), vertical accessibility (22\%), adhan sound (12\%), and room size (4\%). The study also showed that attachment to mosque varied among different age groups based on socio-demographics. In conclusion, a design and disaster management system are needed to optimize the function of worship and evacuation in mosque.},
