	abstract = {Domestic ‘Food waste’ is often preventable. It is influenced by a complex interplay of contextual and cultural factors, which can have contradictory demands. Despite its significance, domestic food waste remains under-researched and food disposal decisions, even less so.Employing a realistic scenario-based approach within a questionnaire, this study investigated the likelihood of a nationally representative sample of UK consumers (n=2046) throwing away a variety of foods and influences on this: notably optimistic bias, place attachment, trust in government and food involvement, which to the authors’ knowledge have not been explored in this context previously.Results showed large proportions of food may be thrown away and unveiled the multifaceted nature of food waste decisions and the significant role of less-studied predictors, including optimistic bias. The scenario-based approach has provided deeper insights into the dynamics of consumer food waste generation and contributes to the UN SDG of 'Responsible Consumption and Production, SDG12.},
