	issn = {1095-9203},
	doi = {10.1126/science.2296719},
	abstract = {Discusses priming, a nonconscious form of human memory, which is concerned with perceptual identification of words and objects and which has only recently been recognized as separate from other forms of memory or memory systems. Experimental evidence is converging for the proposition that priming is an expression of a perceptual representation system (PRS) that operates at a pre-semantic level; the PRS emerges early in development, and access to it lacks the kind of flexibility characteristic of other cognitive memory systems. It is suggested that this PRS exists separately from, but interacts closely with, other memory systems. Conceptual priming, however, seems to be based on the operations of semantic memory. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2018 APA, all rights reserved)},
