	abstract = {Tested the hypothesis that attention must be directed serially to each stimulus in a display whenever conjunctions of more than one separable feature are needed to characterize or distinguish the possible objects presented. Several predictions were tested in 9 experiments that included visual search, texture segregation, and identification and localization, and used both separable dimensions (shape and color) and local elements or parts of figures (e.g., lines and curves in letters) as the features to be integrated into complex wholes: 51 Ss were studied. Results are in general consistent with the hypothesis. They offer a new set of criteria for distinguishing separable from integral features and a new rationale for predicting which tasks will show attention limits and which will not. (43 ref) (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)},
